.TH TRASH 1 "November 22, 2026" "trash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash \- safe rm replacement with recovery
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIPATH\fR...
.br
.B trash
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash
moves files and directories into a private trash store and can restore or
permanently purge them later. It never overwrites an existing file on restore;
conflicts receive a \fB~N\fR suffix.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B put
Move files and directories to the trash. This is the default when no command is
given.
.TP
.B list
List items trashed from the current directory. Use \fB-r\fR for the subtree and
\fB--all\fR for the whole trash.
.TP
.B restore
Restore the newest item, a path, an id, or a unique id prefix.
\fB--from-cwd\fR restores exactly what \fBtrash list\fR shows.
.TP
.B purge
Permanently delete matching items. This cannot be undone.
.TP
.B empty
Permanently delete everything in the trash.
.TP
.B where
Show the trash location and item count.
.TP
.B doctor
Report broken trash items. With \fB--repair\fR, fix id mismatches,
pending/restoring state, invalid metadata fields, and corrupt metadata when
the payload still exists; with \fB--doctor-purge\fR, remove broken items.
.TP
.B help
Show help for a command.
.TP
.B version
Show the program version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-graveyard \fIPATH\fR
Use \fIPATH\fR as the trash store. Defaults to \fB$GRAVEYARD\fR,
\fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/trash\fR, or \fB~/.local/share/trash\fR.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Required to trash a directory. With \fBlist\fR, include the current directory
subtree. With \fBrestore --from-cwd\fR, restore the same subtree.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-inspect
Show a preview and ask for confirmation before trashing.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation. Required for non-interactive \fBempty\fR and
multi-item \fBpurge\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-all
With \fBlist\fR, show the whole trash. With \fBrestore\fR or \fBpurge\fR,
operate on every match for each path.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable JSON where supported.
.TP
.B \-\-long, \-\-full\-id
Show full ids, kinds, and full timestamps in \fBlist\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit \fBlist\fR output to \fIN\fR items.
.TP
.B \-\-sort \fIMODE\fR
Sort \fBlist\fR by \fBdate\fR, \fBsize\fR, \fBpath\fR, or \fBkind\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-kind \fIKIND\fR
Filter by \fBfile\fR, \fBdir\fR, \fBsymlink\fR, or \fBother\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-grep \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter by a substring of the original path.
.TP
.B \-\-older\-than \fIAGE\fR
Filter or purge items older than an age such as \fB30d\fR, \fB12h\fR,
\fB5m\fR, or \fB10s\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-keep \fIN\fR
With \fBempty\fR, keep the newest \fIN\fR items.
.TP
.B \-\-select
With \fBrestore\fR, interactively choose one item.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
With \fBdoctor\fR, repair metadata where possible.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor\-purge
With \fBdoctor\fR, permanently remove broken items.
.TP
.B \-\-from\-cwd
Restore the items shown by \fBtrash list\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-wait
With \fBempty\fR, wait for permanent deletion instead of deferring GC.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Explain what is happening.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress non-error output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colours. The \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable is also honoured.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sync
Disable fsync durability. This is unsafe: data may be lost after a crash.
Use only for disposable stores or filesystems that cannot fsync directories.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GRAVEYARD
Default trash store.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Used to derive \fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/trash\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colours when set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on operational failure, 2 on invalid arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
trash report.txt
.PP
trash -r build/
.PP
trash list -r
.PP
trash restore --from-cwd
.PP
trash purge --all ./report.txt
.PP
trash empty --yes
.PP
trash empty --older-than 30d --yes
.PP
trash purge --older-than 7d --dry-run
.PP
trash doctor --repair
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1)
